{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat inputs/P.txt'","aggregated_output":"Title: Uplink-Completion-Triggered Edge-GPU Inference for Multi-Agent Cooperative Perception

Abstract: This paper investigates the coupling between wireless input completion and graphics processing unit (GPU) execution in centralized multi-agent cooperative perception. Specifically, beyond the conceptual treatment of completion-triggered overlap, a complete execution path is realized and validated on a physical GPU for a cooperative-perception deep neural network (DNN). Each encoder branch is released immediately upon completion of its corresponding input transmission, while the original fusion dependencies and inference mapping are preserved. The resulting release-triggered communication computation coupling (RTCC) propagates validated wireless completion events through host-to-device (H2D) staging, CUDA synchronization, and dependency-preserving branch dispatch, while remaining compatible with causal wireless schedulers. Experiments combining trace-driven wireless arrivals, physical GPU execution, and measured-DAG evaluation show that RTCC reduces complete-detection latency across different communication loads and schedulers, while preserving identical detection outputs and average-precision performance relative to conventional execution.
","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}

{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat ledger.jsonl'","aggregated_output":"{"seq": 1, "at": "2026-09-18T14:16:48Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Q (Learning-Augmented Algorithms survey) provides: prediction interfaces, error measures, consistency-robustness trade-offs, construction mechanisms across 5 domains. Key insight: formal guarantees separate from empirical evidence, explicit treatment of prediction cost and endogenous error. P (RTCC) provides: reactive completion-triggered GPU dispatch for multi-agent cooperative perception, preserves fusion dependencies and detection outputs, reduces latency vs conventional execution. Current P mechanism triggers on actual transmission completion, not predictions.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 2, "at": "2026-09-18T14:16:56Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "idea", "text": "Abstract scan hypothesis: Apply Q prediction framework to P RTCC by predicting transmission-completion times or channel states for proactive GPU dispatch. ASSESSMENT: Partially viable but needs sharpening. SUPPORT: (1) Q provides prediction-interface templates and error measures applicable to wireless timing. (2) P reactive trigger on actual completion leaves room for prediction-based improvement. (3) Measurable latency metrics in P enable evaluation. CONCERNS: (1) Wireless channel prediction accuracy may be insufficient for tight GPU-CUDA synchronization. (2) Q emphasizes prediction cost - overhead may negate latency gains in real-time perception. (3) P requires preserving causal wireless schedulers - predictions must not violate causality. (4) \"Learning-augmented scheduling\" alone is not novel; contribution must be in the specific RTCC-GPU coupling.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
